.TH "TDDA DIFF" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda diff manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda diff\fR — compare csv or parquet files

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda diff\fR [\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fP,...]
            [\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fP,...  ]
            [\fB--horizontal\fR] [\fB-H\fR] [\fB--vertical\fR] [\fB-V\fR]
            [\fB--find-md\fR] [\fB--no-md\fR]
            [\fB--maxdiffs\fR \fIN\fP] [\fB--key\fR \fIFIELD\fP]
            [\fB--mono\fR] [\fB--bw\fR] [\fB--colours\fR \fICOLOURS\fP] [\fB-c\fR \fICOLOURS\fP]
            [\fB--dps\fR \fIN\fP]  [\fB--precision\fR \fIN\fP]
            [\fB--AE\fR] [\fB--LR\fR] [\fB--angles\fR] [\fB--pm\fR]
            [\fB--prefixes\fR \fIPREFIXES\fP]
            [\fB-N\fR] [\fB--no-config\fR]
            [\fB--strict\fR] [\fB--medium\fR] [\fB--loose\fR] [\fB--permissive\fR]
            \fILEFT\fP \fIRIGHT\fP

.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS
\fILEFT\fP The first dataset to be compared, as a parquet or flat file
       (e.g. CSV), optionally using \fB:\fR format to specify flat-file metadata
       (see the help for \fBtdda serial\fR).
       (Normally thought of as left or actual)

.PP
\fIRIGHT\fP The second dataset to be compared as a parquet or flat file
        (e.g. CSV), optionally using \fB:\fR format to specify flat-file metadata
        (see the help for \fBtdda serial\fR).
        (Normally thought of as right, expected, reference, etc.)

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda diff\fR command compares two tabular datasets in CSV or Parquet
files and shows some or all differences. It uses the same underlying
functionality as the \fBtdda.referencetest\fR assertions such as
\fBassertDataFramesEqual\fR, and provides similar control over what
differences to consider, e.g. which fields, and strictness of type and
numeric comparisons. It also provides a number of options for controlling
the display of differences.

.PP
By default, comparisons are row-based and consider all fields (columns),
as typed values after reading.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB*\fR indicates options that are the default behaviours

.PP
\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fP,...
.br
  Check only these fields (comma-separated list)

.PP
\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fP,...
.br
  Check all fields except these (comma-separated list)

.PP
\fB--horizontal\fR, \fB-H\fR
.br
  Horizontal display (left and right, side by side)

.PP
\fB--vertical\fR, \fB-V\fR
.br
  Vertical display (left above right)

.PP
\fB--find-md\fR
.br
  Attempt to find associated metadata for flat files automatically,
  without requiring \fB:\fR colon syntax in the path.

.PP
\fB--no-md\fR, \fB--no-find-md\fR
.br
  Do not attempt to find associated metadata for flat files (default).

.PP
\fB--key\fR \fIFIELD\fP
.br
  Use this field as a join key when reporting differences.

.PP
\fB--maxdiffs\fR \fIN\fP
.br
  Maximum number of differences to show.

.PP
\fB--mono\fR
.br
  Show monochrome output with different values in bold
  and shared values dimmed.

.PP
\fB--bw\fR
.br
  Show black and white output with different values in bold and shared
  values in the terminal's default style.

.PP
\fB--colours\fR \fICOLOURS\fP, \fB-c\fR \fICOLOURS\fP
.br
  Use colours specified e.g. \fB-c red-blue\fR

.PP
\fB--dps\fR \fIN\fP
.br
  Number of decimal places to show for floating-point values.
  Also sets precision if not specified separately.

.PP
\fB--precision\fR \fIN\fP
.br
  Precision for floating point comparisons. Two floats \fBa\fR and \fBb\fR will be
  considered equal if \fBabs(a - b) < 1e-\fR\fIN\fP\&.

.PP
\fB--AE\fR
.br
  Use \fBA:\fR and \fBE:\fR as labels for the two datasets (actual/expected)

.PP
\fB--LR\fR
.br
  Use \fBL:\fR and \fBR:\fR as labels for the two datasets (left/right)

.PP
\fB--angles\fR
.br
  Use \fB<\fR and \fB>\fR as labels for the two datasets

.PP
\fB--pm\fR
.br
  Use \fB+\fR and \fB-\fR as labels for the two datasets

.PP
\fB--prefixes\fR \fIPREFIXES\fP
.br
  Use prefixes specified as labels for the two datasets
  e.g. \fB--prefixes "actual:-ref:"\fR or \fB"actual: -ref: "\fR to include spaces

.PP
\fB-N\fR, \fB--no-config\fR
.br
  Use default configuration (ignore \fB~/.tdda.toml\fR)

.PP
\fB--strict\fR
.br
  Use strict type comparisons

.PP
\fB--medium\fR
.br
  Use medium-strictness type comparisons

.PP
\fB--loose\fR
.br
  Use loose (permissive) type comparisons

.PP
\fB--permissive\fR
.br
  Use loose (permissive) type comparisons

.PP
\fB--pandas\fR, \fB--pd\fR          Use Pandas as DataFrame engine. *
.br
\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pl\fR          Use Polars as DataFrame engine.
.br
\fB--backend\fR, \fB-B\fR \fIBACKEND\fP   Backend choice for Pandas
.br
                        (when dataframe engine is Pandas)
.br
                            \fBn\fR for numpy_nullable *
.br
                            \fBa\fR for pyarrow
.br
                            \fBo\fR for original.

.PP
\fB--help\fR, \fB-?\fR, \fB--?\fR
.br
  Show help on \fBtdda diff\fR\&.

.SH EXAMPLES
Data suitable for all examples can be obtained with

.PP
\fBtdda examples diff\fR

.PP
1) \fBtdda diff a.csv a.csv\fR

.PP
This is the simplest form of the command. It will read \fBa.csv\fR and
convert it to a data frame, using the default back end (Pandas).

.PP
2) \fBtdda diff a.csv b.csv --vertical\fR

.PP
Compare two CSV files, stacking left and right values vertically
rather than side by side. Useful when there are many columns or
long values.

.PP
3) \fBtdda diff before.parquet after.parquet --key Income,Expenditure\fR

.PP
Compare two Parquet files using a composite join key. The fields
\fBIncome\fR and \fBExpenditure\fR must form a primary key in both datasets.
Rows are matched by key rather than by position.

.PP
4) \fBtdda diff actual.csv expected.csv --AE --bw\fR

.PP
Compare two CSV files using \fBA:\fR and \fBE:\fR as markers for actual and
expected, with monochrome bold highlighting instead of colour.

.PP
5) \fBtdda diff foo.csv: bar.csv:\fR

.PP
Compare two CSV files, asking TDDA to find associated metadata files
for each using naming conventions (e.g. \fB@.serial\fR or
\fBfoo-metadata.json\fR in the same directory).

.PP
6) \fBtdda diff foo.csv bar.txt:money.serial\fR

.PP
Compare \fBfoo.csv\fR (loaded with default settings) against \fBbar.txt\fR,
using \fBmoney.serial\fR as the metadata file describing its format.

.PP
7) \fBtdda diff a.parquet b.csv --loose --dps 3\fR

.PP
Compare a Parquet file against a CSV file with loose type matching
and floating-point values compared to 3 decimal places.
